\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Square positivity need not imply real Hermiticity}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{In the real algebra #{A=\mathbb R[\varepsilon]/(\varepsilon^2)},
each element is uniquely #{x+y\varepsilon}. Define
#{(x+y\varepsilon)^*=x-y\varepsilon}. For any fixed real #{c\ne0}, let
##{\omega_c(x+y\varepsilon)=x+cy.}
Then #{\omega_c} is normalized and nonnegative on every #{a^*a}, but is
not a [real algebraic state](fdip-0024), because it is not Hermitian.}
\proof{
\p{The operation is real linear, fixes the unit and squares to the
identity. Multiplication gives
#{(x+y\varepsilon)(u+v\varepsilon)=xu+(xv+yu)\varepsilon}; applying
star negates the second coefficient, as does multiplying the starred
factors. Since the algebra is commutative, this is also the required
order-reversing product law. For #{a=x+y\varepsilon},
#{a^*a=(x-y\varepsilon)(x+y\varepsilon)=x^2}.
Thus #{\omega_c(a^*a)=x^2\geq0}, and #{\omega_c(1)=1}.
But #{\omega_c(\varepsilon^*)=-c\ne c=\omega_c(\varepsilon)}.}
}
\p{This direct calculation distinguishes square positivity from the extra
real Hermiticity condition. Square positivity is defined in
\citet{Definition 3.9, p. 11}{waldmann2004states}.
It is separate from the [characteristic-two group-algebra example](fdip-000N):
the present algebra and functional are over #{\mathbb R}.}
